00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.410
So with that, let's start
off with the first module,

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:02.850
network topology.

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.070
Network topology is
a visualization off

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:07.320
your global GCP deployment.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.470
It shows actual communications
between various networking

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.960
nodes, like compute instances,
to the public internet

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:18.600
and also to network services
like load balancers, NAT, et

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:19.560
cetera.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.860
And it also shows you all of
the associated performance

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:26.110
metrics for all of those edges.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:29.300
And all of these metrics, as
well as the topology itself,

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.430
we are able to capture and
track over a period of time--

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:33.780
over six weeks--

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.820
so that you can actually
see how each of these

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:40.100
evolve over a period of time
and generate insights from that.

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:44.470
Next, let's move to how you
can use network topology.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.170
These are some of
the common use cases

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:49.320
that we saw our customers
using network topology.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.200
The first one is
network monitoring.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.200
So this is where
customers made sure

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.260
that the topology that they
viewed here within the Network

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:02.735
Intelligence Center was in
line with what the intent was

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:04.110
by the network
team, as they were

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.730
deploying that infrastructure.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:10.830
And so that could mean
things like, for example,

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.550
if you want all of
your compute instances

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:17.513
to talk to the public
internet through load balances

00:05:17.513 --> 00:05:18.930
and not directly,
that's something

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:23.190
that you can very easily
check using this product.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.980
Or you can very quickly check if
all of your, say, for example,

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:29.340
Asia users are being served
from the closest region to them

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:30.990
or not.

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:34.440
The second use case is around
network troubleshooting.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.900
So network topology has a
very fast and quick filtering

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.960
capability, where in
case, for example, you

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:45.140
are troubleshooting as
best network issue related

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:46.820
to an instance or
a load balance,

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:50.090
you can filter for
that specific instance

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:53.180
and view all of the
traffic, as well as

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:54.920
metrics, associated with that.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.350
We have the instance
very quickly,

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:02.480
as well as see what that looked
like at the point of time

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.020
of the incident, because you're
saving the topology itself

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:08.340
over a period of time.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:12.880
The third use case is
architecture optimization.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:14.580
So in our architecture
optimization,

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.890
once the customers
actually understand

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.320
what the architecture
looks like,

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.540
they can now very quickly
perform optimizations

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.360
at cost or performance.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.550
For example, being
able to understand

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.040
what your traffic looks
like between various regions

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.920
can help you optimize
egress traffic.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:35.490
The next one is tracking
workload migration.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:38.820
So as you're moving your
resources onto the Cloud,

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.580
topology provides
you a very easy

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.060
to understand how your
deployment is evolving,

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:47.080
what are the performance
metrics associated,

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:49.170
so that you can
tweak your deployment

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:52.530
architecture to optimize
for performance, or cost,

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.300
or availability.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.340
The fifth one is
around compliance--

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.530
checks and audits.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:02.580
Export this topology to be
able to use some of this

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.910
as part of your SOC
audits, without requiring

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:07.320
any third-party tools.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:09.100
This is a very quick
capture of what

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:10.320
network topology looks like.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.670
On the top, you
have countries which

00:07:13.670 --> 00:07:16.560
have captured external
clients, public internet.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:17.935
In the middle are
load balancers,

00:07:17.935 --> 00:07:20.185
which you can click through
to understand all the load

00:07:20.185 --> 00:07:21.380
balancers you have deployed.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.400
And at the bottom
are GCP regions

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:25.700
where you have your
compute instances deployed.

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:29.280
And you can drill down all
the way to the VM instance.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:30.775
All of these edges
capture metrics,

00:07:30.775 --> 00:07:32.400
and you can click
through to understand

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.110
exactly how those
metrics evolved

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:37.130
for each of those edges.

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:41.080
Now, let's move on to the
next product with the Network

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:43.450
Intelligence Center called
Performance Dashboard.

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:47.260
Our Performance Dashboard
provides real-time performance

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:51.970
metrics, like latency and
packet loss, for your project

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:53.740
and for your set of VMs.

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:58.840
So this reflects the customer's
actual experience within GCP.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:03.340
We look at all of the
VM-to-VM performance metrics

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:05.980
and aggregate it to
a zone level to be

00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:12.570
able to show you what the
inter-zone latency and packet

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:18.130
loss looks like for your
project and your environment.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:21.420
So what that means is whenever
there is a, for example,

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:23.680
you're investigating
an application issue,

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:26.920
you can very quickly understand
if this is a network level

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:30.560
issue or not and take
a call based upon that.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:35.419
These metrics will be available
over the Project Performance

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:37.130
dashboard, which is now GA.

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:40.970
It is part of your Network
Intelligence Center console.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:44.480
It will also be available as
part of our network topology,

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.730
as part of the edges
that we just saw.

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:50.473
As well as our
metrics are concerned,

00:08:50.473 --> 00:08:51.890
we are cloud
monitoring, where you

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:56.780
can set up alerts as per your
requirements and use cases.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:59.950
Now, I will move on to show
the demo of the Performance

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:01.240
Dashboard.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:03.840
Now, let's get
started with the demo.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.030
So the way to get to
Performance Dashboard

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:08.280
is you go to your console.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.490
Under Networking, you will
find Network Intelligence

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:13.020
and Performance Dashboard there.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:14.220
You can click through that.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.080
Performance Dashboard
is a GA product,

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:18.450
so you should be able to
view this at your end.

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:22.770
And what you'll see here
is packet loss and latency

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:26.020
for your set of VMs and your
specific project itself.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.660
So it's your experience
of the network.

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:34.440
And on this chart here, you
will see the average packet loss

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:38.190
across all of your zone pairs
where you have VMs deployed,

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:41.280
and also the packet loss
for the highest loss zone

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.990
pair for any given sample.

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.560
So now, if I want to
investigate further,

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:51.410
let's click through and
zoom in on this timeframe

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:57.520
and see what's happening
at this specific timeframe.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:01.360
Now, when I clicked on that,
a two-by-two chart matrix

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:02.500
shows up.

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:04.060
And you'll see
all of these zones

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:07.780
are basically the zones where
you have your VMs deployed.

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:09.940
And here, I see that
there's a specific packet

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:14.950
loss recorded between Asia
east 1B and Europe west 2C.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:17.350
Now, I can click
through further here

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:22.240
to understand for this specific
zone pair, what the packet

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:24.590
loss was over a period of time.

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:28.200
And this metric is
recorded over six weeks,

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:31.270
so you can view
that over six weeks.

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:34.600
Now, I can zoom in further here
and understand that this packet

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:39.430
loss was recorded at 9:41.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:44.680
And at 9:40 and
9:42, it was at zero.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:46.960
So what this enables
you to answer

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.285
is, whenever there is any
application where issue

00:10:49.285 --> 00:10:51.430
is happening, you can go
back here and understand

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:53.380
if this was related
to a network issue

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:56.170
or not, and also how
long did it last.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:58.330
Now going back
here, similarly, you

00:10:58.330 --> 00:11:01.690
can access latency
information as well.

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:05.260
And you have similar charts
available for latency.

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:07.390
And also you can
see median latency

00:11:07.390 --> 00:11:09.340
across all of these
zone pairs where

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:14.110
you have your VMs deployed,
at any point in time.

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:19.390
With that, let's move back to
the rest of the presentation

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:22.110
and the other modules.

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:27.030
With that, next, I want to also
call out the Live Public Google

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:30.360
Cloud Inter-region Performance
Dashboard that we recently

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.040
launched.

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:35.640
This shows inter-region
performance metrics, latency,

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:40.170
and throughput for all of
our inter-region traffic

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:42.670
using PerfKit Benchmarker.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:47.190
PerfKit Benchmarker is
an open-source toolkit

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:51.510
provided by Google, but it
has 100 plus contributors.

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:55.050
And the key thing to this is
it utilizes the common aspects

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:58.410
of performance testing, like
ping, network, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:01.470
But what it does it is it
automates a lot of the process

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:02.430
that I'm testing.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:06.840
So it automates a process
around provisioning your VMs,

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.210
setting up the VPC,
setting up firewall rules,

00:12:09.210 --> 00:12:12.060
as well as at the end
of the test, [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.280
entire infrastructure.

00:12:14.280 --> 00:12:20.400
And this dashboard also provides
a tutorial into how exactly

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:22.620
you can reproduce
any of these metrics.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.290
We are providing this use
so that you can make calls

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:33.450
around where to deploy your
resources so that you can

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:35.970
optimize for performance
within Google Cloud

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:39.240
and provide that visibility
to make those decisions.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:43.460
With that, I will
hand it over to Gargi.

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:45.630
GARGI ADHAV: Thank you, Manasa.

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:47.750
So the next module
we will talk about

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:50.130
is called connectivity test.

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:53.690
So one of the biggest pinpoints
that we heard from customers

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:56.900
during our research, and
like Manasa mentioned,

00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:00.530
was the lack of
troubleshooting capabilities.

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:03.200
And when we did a
survey of the customers,

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:08.270
it turns out that almost
75% of our network failures

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:10.790
and performance
issues are resulting

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:13.190
from configuration errors.

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:17.270
And once these network
failures happen,

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:21.610
it is really hard to pinpoint
and troubleshoot a root cause

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:24.740
as to where the
problem was happening.

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:26.450
And that itself
takes a long time,

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:28.790
and it increases
the outage period.

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:30.920
So one of the biggest
requests from customers

00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:32.840
was that if you
could just tell us

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:36.050
whether the root cause of
the failure is at Google,

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:38.360
or is it us, or
is it the network.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:40.880
That itself would
be a huge value.

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:43.160
The other problem was
that it's very hard

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:45.680
to understand that
when any of your admins

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:48.590
are making configuration
changes, what is

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:50.380
going to be the impact of it--

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:52.610
whether these are rock
solid config changes,

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:54.750
or is something going to fail?

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:57.110
So The What of the
connectivity test

00:13:57.110 --> 00:14:02.060
is a tool to help you
diagnose connectivity issues--

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:04.820
before you can
actually do it, or even

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:06.530
after you have done it--

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:08.690
saying, these are the
configs that have changed,

00:14:08.690 --> 00:14:13.040
and as a result, these are the
failures of between a source

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:15.720
or destination, or to
even validate that,

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:18.030
yes, this the flow that
you were expecting,

00:14:18.030 --> 00:14:20.640
and it is doing it
as you expected.

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:21.840
How do we do it?

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:24.920
So we don't actually run
a ping or a trace route,

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:28.700
so this is complete static
modeling of your network using

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:33.780
all your configuration files,
and your routing information,

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:35.730
and so on.

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:39.800
So here's an example
of a connectivity test.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:42.560
You can have source
and [? S ?] protocols

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:45.200
or protocol and ports.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:47.750
Either you can
specify an IP address

00:14:47.750 --> 00:14:50.580
as a source and destination,
or you can have VMs--

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:54.080
a source VM and a des VM,
with their respective ports,

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:56.640
which is optional, and
also protocol feeding,

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:58.300
which is optional.

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:01.790
And what we give to you is
a step-by-step visualization

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:04.790
about what pack your
packet would be taking

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:07.750
and what configuration
it would be hitting.

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:10.790
So in this case, it is
starting from a VM instance.

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:14.690
There is an egress firewall
that it hits, which it passes.

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:16.340
Then there is a
static route look up

00:15:16.340 --> 00:15:18.980
that happens, which it passes.

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:23.600
And then there is a NAT lookup,
which is where it fails.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:26.330
Maybe there is a
missing NAT route.

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:29.510
So here are a few examples
of connectivity tests

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:31.850
that we have shown, like
different paths that it

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:33.320
can take.

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:37.550
The first one is it goes
through a firewall rule.

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:38.990
It is successful.

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:41.750
And if there is no
reason for the failure

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:43.400
and it says the
packet is dropped,

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:47.780
it could also be that it goes
outside your IAM policy domain

00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:48.590
that it allows you.

00:15:48.590 --> 00:15:51.380
And you may want to reach
out to your super admin who

00:15:51.380 --> 00:15:54.680
has the policies because
[INAUDIBLE] between both

00:15:54.680 --> 00:15:57.680
of you, you could
understand the reason.

00:15:57.680 --> 00:15:59.970
The second example,
in this case,

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.880
it is going through
a firewall rule

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.880
and then it is getting dropped.

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:09.200
In the third
example, it is it is

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:13.160
going through a load balancer
and then it is getting dropped.

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:15.200
And the fourth one is
a successful example

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:19.070
that we have taken, where
it goes to a VPN tunnel

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:21.890
and then it all
comes in and will

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:25.160
show you the different steps,
just a traceroute would.

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:28.910
Except that in this
case, a traceroute

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:31.250
shows IP addresses,
versus here, you

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:33.440
see all the steps of
the configurations

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:37.820
that would be executed on that
packet-- be it a VPN tunnel,

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:40.580
be it a routing lookup,
be it a NAT rule look up,

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:41.960
or a firewall rule.

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:46.710
And in case of a NAT or firewall
rule failure or even a success,

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:49.160
it will tell you exactly
which rule it hit

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:52.260
and why it was a
success or a failure.

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:54.110
So you could go
back in real time

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:56.900
and say, oh, this firewall
rule is accidentally

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:57.840
blocking this packet.

00:16:57.840 --> 00:16:59.300
And that's not what I intended.

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.820
You go change that, and then
you will see immediately

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:04.500
that your packet
flow is successful.

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:07.609
So let's talk about
the next module.

00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:09.950
This is called
Firewall Insights.

00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:14.450
So one of the other challenges
that we heard from many of you

00:17:14.450 --> 00:17:18.410
is around firewalls and the
management of firewall rules.

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:21.050
Several people have
thousands and thousands

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:24.440
of firewall rules, and if
they wanted to optimize that,

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:26.147
they did not know
where to start.

00:17:26.147 --> 00:17:27.980
Because usually what
we heard was that there

00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:29.090
is a series of admins.

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:32.240
Some security admin in the past
must have created this firewall

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:35.040
rule, and they went ahead
to a different rule,

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:37.560
and new security admins came on.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:40.100
And we said, then
how do you figure out

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:41.400
which rules are still active?

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.510
They said, oh, we do not
delete firewall rules.

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:46.370
We just add them.

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:49.820
So that makes the firewall
rules very unmanageable,

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:51.870
and you don't even
know where to start.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:55.700
So as a result, you end
up with a massive volume

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:56.990
of firewall rules.

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:59.270
And sometimes there is
over-granting of rules,

00:17:59.270 --> 00:18:02.390
because you just granted at a
higher level IP address range,

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:06.140
even though you may only be
partially using some of it.

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:08.480
There is also human
error, of course.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:10.200
And there is no
way to figure out,

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:14.570
unless there is an actual
traffic drop, that something

00:18:14.570 --> 00:18:16.700
bad had happened.

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:19.280
And then the other thing,
like we talked about,

00:18:19.280 --> 00:18:21.440
is outdated rules,
because people

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:24.080
are too afraid to change
them, mainly going back

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:26.060
to they don't know
what else could break

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:28.130
and what was the
intention of this rule,

00:18:28.130 --> 00:18:30.110
for why it was created.

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:32.810
So Firewall Insights took
on all of these problems.

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:36.170
And we said, OK, let's tackle
that by applying intelligence

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:40.307
to the data that we have and
the configurations that we have.

00:18:40.307 --> 00:18:42.140
So one of the first
things that we have done

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:46.760
is give you visibility
into usage metrics based

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:48.350
on each firewall
rule, like what has

00:18:48.350 --> 00:18:52.560
been the hit rate in the past
x number of days, and so on.

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:56.300
And you can see the hit
count, the timestamp,

00:18:56.300 --> 00:18:57.690
and you can export this.

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.020
This data is exported
to Stackdriver where

00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:02.060
you can set your own alerts.

00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:06.020
The other part is,
through analysis

00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:09.710
and applying some symbolic
analysis on the config data,

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:12.770
we are able to show you what is
the hierarchical relationship

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:14.240
among different tools.

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:16.540
Which rule is
shadowing another rule?

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:19.400
Like for example, there
is a high-priority slash

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:24.010
16 rule, and then there are
three low-priority slash 24

00:19:24.010 --> 00:19:27.640
rules, are obviously
being shadowed.

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:32.120
Slash 16 is shadowing
the lower priority rules.

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:34.100
And as a result,
you could easily

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:38.390
get rid of the underlying
three allow rules,

00:19:38.390 --> 00:19:41.060
because you have an
overarching allow

00:19:41.060 --> 00:19:48.650
rule that is addressing this
allow functionality or desire.

00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:51.560
So that's a very simple
example of optimization.

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:55.100
But then there are other
more complex examples.

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:56.520
So we analyze all of that.

00:19:56.520 --> 00:20:01.220
And we show you the shadowing
and shadowed relationship

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:02.390
among the rules.

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:06.230
And then, as a result, we allow
you to strengthen your firewall

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:10.010
control by even
recommending to you

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:13.400
or showing you insights on
where some of your security

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:15.410
could be tightened,
by showing you

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:19.100
that these are the only
rules which are being hit.

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:21.060
So there are two
ends of the spectrum.

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:22.970
On one end of the
spectrum, you are

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:25.640
able to optimize
your firewall rules.

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:27.530
And on the other
end of the spectrum,

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.860
you're able to strengthen
your security by making

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:34.070
the rules more specific.

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:38.390
And you could do both, based
on what your desire is.

00:20:38.390 --> 00:20:41.130
Now, we'll go to the demo
for Firewall Insights.

00:20:44.465 --> 00:20:48.210
Firewall Insights provides
you three main things today.

00:20:48.210 --> 00:20:50.630
Firstly, it shows you the
different relationships

00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:52.270
among your firewall rules--

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:55.400
the shadowing, shadowed
conflicting relationships.

00:20:55.400 --> 00:20:59.990
Secondly, it shows you which of
your deny rules have been hit,

00:20:59.990 --> 00:21:02.570
and thirdly, which
of your allowed rules

00:21:02.570 --> 00:21:06.050
have not been hit
in the last 60 days.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:08.510
As we discussed,
firewall rules can

00:21:08.510 --> 00:21:11.030
be added by different
people over time,

00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:13.640
causing new rules to be
accidentally overshadowed

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:17.060
by existing rules,
or vice versa.

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:21.420
Without any automated detection,
this is really hard to do.

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:26.600
So for example, if there is a
VM 1 in a 10.1 slash 24 subnet,

00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:31.550
which cannot reach VM 2 in a
10.2 slash 24 subnet when you

00:21:31.550 --> 00:21:35.330
intended it to, the first thing
you would do is run a ping

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:35.870
test.

00:21:35.870 --> 00:21:40.355
So you go to VM 1 and
you can ping 10.2.0.2

00:21:40.355 --> 00:21:41.230
and see what happens.

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:45.910
The ping failed.

00:21:45.910 --> 00:21:48.160
So let's see what could
be the root cause of this.

00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:53.560
So to troubleshoot,
you would go to VM2

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:55.910
and view the network
details to see

00:21:55.910 --> 00:21:59.900
what are the configurations
that are applied on VM2.

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:02.350
And you see, oh,
there is a 10.1 slash

00:22:02.350 --> 00:22:07.400
16 deny firewall rule which
is shadowing a 10.1 slash 24

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:10.430
allow firewall rule,
naturally causing your traffic

00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:13.760
to be blocked and thus break.

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:16.270
There could be multiple
such instances that

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:18.260
are in your firewall rules.

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:19.850
And if you have
thousands of them,

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:23.060
it's really hard to
automatically detect and find

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:24.690
these conflicts.

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:27.980
So Firewall Insights in
Network Intelligence Center

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:29.900
provides you exactly that.

00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:32.990
It gives you a list of all your
shadowed rules in your firewall

00:22:32.990 --> 00:22:37.610
conflicts, in your VPC, that
you can audit and figure out

00:22:37.610 --> 00:22:41.570
which ones to clean and
keep, or which ones to keep.

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:43.910
So a single rule
or multiple rules

00:22:43.910 --> 00:22:47.510
can be blocked by multiple or
a combination of other rules.

00:22:47.510 --> 00:22:51.800
In this case, an allow
rule to TCP ports 18243

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:55.235
are being blocked by, or
shadowed by, or denied

00:22:55.235 --> 00:23:00.650
by old rule, actually causing
this breakage to happen.

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:05.450
So you may want to proactively
audit all those shadowed rules

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:08.960
in your firewall
conflicts and figure out

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:14.000
which ones match your
traffic needs and intent,

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.520
and which ones don't and
you don't want to keep,

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:19.480
and clean those up.

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:22.600
The second powerful thing that
Firewall Insights provides

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:24.760
is a list of allow
rules that have not

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:26.950
been hit in the last six weeks.

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:30.010
So you could come here to view
a full list of these rules.

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:36.210
So as you can see, there are
multiple rules that have not

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:37.680
been hit in the last six weeks.

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:43.760
And if you ordered
these, these could

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:46.330
be rules left over
by past applications

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:48.900
where someone forgot
to clean them up.

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:53.450
So in this case, there is a
web app 1 which has a firewall

00:23:53.450 --> 00:23:58.310
allow rule for a 0
slash 0 IP address.

00:23:58.310 --> 00:24:00.770
And it's not been hit
in the last six weeks,

00:24:00.770 --> 00:24:03.860
so naturally, web app
1 probably went away

00:24:03.860 --> 00:24:08.280
and this got left over as a
vestige hole in your network.

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:11.570
So reviewing these rules will
help you tighten your security

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:12.740
boundaries.

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:15.330
The third powerful function
that is provided to you

00:24:15.330 --> 00:24:17.510
is the deny rules with hits.

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:20.780
Now, this could point to
unintended, abnormal traffic

00:24:20.780 --> 00:24:21.550
behaviors.

00:24:21.550 --> 00:24:24.950
It could even be attacks
into your network.

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:29.060
And to figure out where these
attacks are coming from,

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:31.550
you could go to the
firewall logs, corresponding

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:36.050
to the firewall rules, and see
where the source of the traffic

00:24:36.050 --> 00:24:38.220
is.

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:41.190
And especially when you have
a large, abnormal behavior,

00:24:41.190 --> 00:24:44.270
you may want to look
into that traffic source.

00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:45.990
Even there is a
misconfiguration,

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:50.760
or there is an attack
coming from those sources.

00:24:50.760 --> 00:24:53.520
In summary, Firewall
Insights gives you

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:56.240
four powerful functions.

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:58.370
Firstly, it gives you
real time visibility

00:24:58.370 --> 00:25:02.060
into your firewall rules
utilization and stats.

00:25:02.060 --> 00:25:04.940
Secondly, it automatically
detects overlapping

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:07.180
and misconfigured
firewall rules.

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:11.000
Thirdly, it helps you optimize
your firewall conflicts

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:13.280
by enabling you to
delete any shadowing,

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:16.520
shadowed, conflicting, or
unused firewall rules that

00:25:16.520 --> 00:25:18.470
do not match your intent.

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:21.350
And finally, it helps
you tighten your security

00:25:21.350 --> 00:25:25.220
boundaries by detecting any
unused vestige rules that

00:25:25.220 --> 00:25:27.980
may be creating holes
in your network.

00:25:27.980 --> 00:25:29.900
Through a combination
of all these,

00:25:29.900 --> 00:25:33.590
Firewall Insights thus helps
you to optimize and maintain

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:36.500
the right set of firewall
rules to protect your cloud

00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:38.380
deployments.

00:25:38.380 --> 00:25:41.980
So to wrap up, we have launched
the Network Intelligence

00:25:41.980 --> 00:25:45.580
Center, which has [INAUDIBLE]
It includes all the four

00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:48.670
modules that Manasa and
I talked about today,

00:25:48.670 --> 00:25:51.730
on network topology, and
performance dashboard,

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:54.430
connectivity test,
and Firewall Insights.

00:25:54.430 --> 00:25:58.660
So as you can see, we
are taking an approach

00:25:58.660 --> 00:26:02.560
of proactive intelligence,
proactively monitoring

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:06.130
and operating your network,
on shortening the outages that

00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:08.450
happen in a network.

00:26:08.450 --> 00:26:12.340
And as a result, then you
come to trust the GCP network

00:26:12.340 --> 00:26:14.780
intelligence more--

00:26:14.780 --> 00:26:18.970
build trust and basically help
you manage your network better.

